{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdevqVYZ8DyBTiSjDCfp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HoQ1hG5YsNqbn3sKpizLGb","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/traccar/bench/trees/traccar-2-harness-haiku/docs/harness/log.md","old_string":"## 2026-11-01: harness built by uw-harness 0.3.0 with claude\n","new_string":"## 2026-11-02: JMAK commands over TCP and SMS\n\nAdded `JmakProtocolEncoder` with a `textChannel` flag: false for the TCP channel (`$id;value;|...#`), true for SMS (`JMAK#action;id;value#...#`, and `JMAK#REBOOT#` for reboot). `JmakProtocol` declares the same 12 commands for data and text and registers the encoder on both channels. Added `Command.TYPE_ALARM_MOVEMENT` (\"alarmMovement\") because no movement alarm type existed. Tests: `JmakProtocolEncoderTest` covers every command over TCP and SMS, plus one run through the production handler order.\n\nLearned: in the TCP pipeline, `NetworkMessageHandler` unwraps `NetworkMessage` before a `StringEncoder` placed in the protocol handlers can see the output, so a String from the encoder would reach the socket unencoded. The TCP encoder therefore returns an ASCII `ByteBuf`. `BaseProtocol` sends the custom command through the encoder only when its type is in the supported data commands, so custom is listed there.\n\nLeft: power removal alarm is mapped to the existing `alarmRemove` type and the movement alarm to the new `alarmMovement` type; both mappings need a human check. The odometer value is sent as given in the command data, with no unit conversion. No test against a real JMAK device.\n\n## 2026-11-01: harness built by uw-harness 0.3.0 with claude\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1675,"cache_read_input_tokens":125780,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1675},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf8f1475-228d-4796-ae58-ad0f616495b9","uuid":"2be8ca18-5578-46cd-b98a-36cf6e544947","timestamp":"2026-11-02T06:48:44.766Z","request_id":"req_011CgdevqCSGsikqL6W8njLe","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01HoQ1hG5YsNqbn3sKpizLGb":{"file_path":"/Users/victor/Repos/unwritten/uw-harness-cases/traccar/bench/trees/traccar-2-harness-haiku/docs/harness/log.md","old_string":"## 2026-11-01: harness built by uw-harness 0.3.0 with claude\n","new_string":"## 2026-11-02: JMAK commands over TCP and SMS\n\nAdded `JmakProtocolEncoder` with a `textChannel` flag: false for the TCP channel (`$id;value;|...#`), true for SMS (`JMAK#action;id;value#...#`, and `JMAK#REBOOT#` for reboot). `JmakProtocol` declares the same 12 commands for data and text and registers the encoder on both channels. Added `Command.TYPE_ALARM_MOVEMENT` (\"alarmMovement\") because no movement alarm type existed. Tests: `JmakProtocolEncoderTest` covers every command over TCP and SMS, plus one run through the production handler order.\n\nLearned: in the TCP pipeline, `NetworkMessageHandler` unwraps `NetworkMessage` before a `StringEncoder` placed in the protocol handlers can see the output, so a String from the encoder would reach the socket unencoded. The TCP encoder therefore returns an ASCII `ByteBuf`. `BaseProtocol` sends the custom command through the encoder only when its type is in the supported data commands, so custom is listed there.\n\nLeft: power removal alarm is mapped to the existing `alarmRemove` type and the movement alarm to the new `alarmMovement` type; both mappings need a human check. The odometer value is sent as given in the command data, with no unit conversion. No test against a real JMAK device.\n\n## 2026-11-01: harness built by uw-harness 0.3.